Runtime console themes (first Newport implementation)
====================================================

Branch: feature/wscons-runtime-themes, based on feature/newport-framed-console.

GENERIC32_IP2x and its 64-bit variants retain NEWPORT_DECOR, so their built-in
appearance is the black-to-#008060 gradient.  With the updated userland and
rc files installed, /etc/rc.d/wsdecor selects the framed image after critical
filesystems mount, before wscons screen configuration and LOGIN.  This also
works when wscons=NO.  Unsupported console drivers are skipped quietly.
The NEWPORT_FRAME configuration remains available for a compiled-in frame
from early console attachment.

To select a theme immediately:

    wsdecorctl /usr/share/wscons/themes/framed.conf
    wsdecorctl /usr/share/wscons/themes/initial.conf

For a persistent custom selection, put a theme in /etc/my-console.conf and
set wsdecor_theme="/etc/my-console.conf" in /etc/rc.conf.  Set wsdecor=NO to
retain the initial appearance throughout startup.  See wsdecorctl(8) for the
configuration format, solid colours, gradients, viewports and image conversion.
The shipped framed theme requires a 1280/1282 by 1024 Newport display.  At
other resolutions its load fails with a diagnostic, leaving the gradient.
The image is not scaled automatically.

Images are converted once using share/wscons/themes/mkwsd.py (Python/Pillow).
The installed utility decodes a bounded palette/run format in userland.  The
kernel accepts only validated raw indices and palette data.  The text panel
must be uniform so the cursor has an exact complementary colour; there are
at most 223 image colours, with terminal colours reserved separately.  The
supplied 161-colour image is lossless.  Regenerate/check it with:

    python3 share/wscons/themes/mkwsd.py \
        sys/arch/sgimips/gio/newport-decor/sgiconsole.png \
        share/wscons/themes/sgiconsole.wsd --check

Viewport resizing resets terminal state, contents and scrollback when rows or
columns change.  It updates every existing screen and tty, and sends SIGWINCH.
A theme or origin change with unchanged rows/columns retains text.  The image
is repainted when returning from graphics mode or switching virtual terminals.
The theme cannot be changed during graphics mode or a pending VT switch.
Only root with a writable descriptor can set it.  Failed validation leaves
both theme and screen dimensions untouched.  VCONS_DRAW_INTR is not supported
by this first viewport implementation.

Kernel/driver implementation
----------------------------

WSDISPLAYIO_GDECOR returns versioned metadata, with physical framebuffer
width/height and a zero user pointer.  WSDISPLAYIO_SDECOR accepts a complete
theme.  A uint64_t image address and fixed-width fields keep the ABI layout
identical for 32-bit and 64-bit callers.  All reserved fields are zero.
The driver validates/copies the image before changing state.  With the kernel
lock held at spltty, it checks wsdisplay_resize_allowed(), asks vcons to
prepare every replacement buffer using non-sleeping allocations, commits the
geometry and palette, calls wsdisplay_resize(), and redraws the active screen.
There are no sleeping allocations in the commit section.  Early output and
serial consoles do not depend on theme files being accessible.

Other accelerated drivers can opt into VCONS_VIEWPORT only if their textops
use ri_xorigin/ri_yorigin and never draw through ri_bits.  They must implement
bounds and palette validation, full background repaint, cursor behaviour,
and mode restoration.  The viewport helper deliberately does not change
fonts or framebuffer mappings.  See README.decor-porting for the other
rendering requirements.  Software rasterisers and deferred rendering need
additional work before they can use this path.

Regression procedure
--------------------

Build the gradient, NEWPORT_FRAME and option-disabled configurations.  Compile
wsdecorctl and newport_theme.c against the updated wsconsio.h in a NetBSD
guest.  In a disposable test guest create ttyE1 with wsconscfg 1 and run
newport_theme as root with ttyE0 as the controlling terminal.  The test checks
invalid versions, reserved fields, types, overflowing/small rectangles, RGB,
image pointers, palette/pixel indices, nonuniform panels, read-only descriptors,
unprivileged setters, graphics mode, resizing both ttys and SIGWINCH.  It
finishes with the original full-screen green gradient.  Use the 8x16 font
and 1280/1282x1024 mode for the hard-coded geometry expectations.

For the userspace parser, build wsdecor_parser.c with the updated sys headers
and run `python3 check_wsdecor_parser.py ./wsdecor_parser`.  It checks valid
image/gradient/solid themes and 19 malformed configurations or image streams.

Apply framed.conf and run newport_decor.c plus the pixel comparisons in
README.newport-frame.  Create screens both before and after selecting a theme;
verify their sizes and switch away/back.  Switch gradient -> frame -> gradient
repeatedly, test a solid colour and a descending custom gradient, and check
that changing colours without changing geometry preserves text.  Reject
malformed theme files, truncated/overlong image runs, out-of-range palettes,
missing files and wrong-resolution images.  GET before/after a failed request
must be identical.  Also boot normally with the rc hook installed and capture
the initial gradient and the later framed startup output.

Use isolated Iris/Prismatic fixtures as described in README.newport-frame.
That document also records a pre-existing composed-frame refresh issue on
large idle solid clears; compare raw framebuffer indices as well.  Emulator
validation does not replace a test on physical Newport hardware.

Validation on 2026-11-02/03
---------------------------

The gradient, compiled-frame and decoration-disabled mipseb kernels built.
The loader compiled inside NetBSD with -Wall -Wextra -Werror.  The version-1
ioctl layout was compiled for MIPS o32 and n64 (736 bytes, palette at offset
64).  All 23 userspace parser cases and 19 kernel checks passed.  The latter
include reserved palette bytes and a tty size pre-set independently of the
emulator geometry: resize still resets the emulator and sends SIGWINCH.

The isolated emulator revisions are those recorded in README.newport-frame.
Prismatic RGB captures and standalone Iris raw-index captures passed the
scroll, erase, cursor, numbered-text and graphics-mode restoration checks
after loading the frame at runtime.  Existing and newly created VTs reported
42x88; switching back to the gradient restored 64x160.  Every RGB pixel outside
the viewport matched the supplied PNG.  A solid #123456 theme filled the
entire display exactly; a descending #ff8040-to-black gradient matched all
112 palette bands.  The final kernel/API checks and normal startup test ran
on Prismatic; Iris rendering checks preceded the final tty-size edge-case fix.
The known idle colour-clear composition limitation remains as documented in
README.newport-frame.

A normal multiuser boot with the installed rc hook showed the green gradient
during early boot, automatically selected the frame, then displayed startup
messages and the login prompt inside the inset rectangle.  Restarting the rc
service restored the configured default after manual theme changes.  Theme
assets were also installed through their source-tree Makefile and the output
was checked against the generated image.

Test captures and the detailed JSON report for this run are under
/tmp/wscons-decor/runtime-boot-initial.png,
/tmp/wscons-decor/runtime-boot-framed.png and
/tmp/wscons-decor/runtime-validation.json.  These are local test artifacts,
not installed files.  No physical hardware or 64-bit kernel runtime test was
performed.
